comments.body:
I think producing an SBOM and distributing it with the wheels via [PEP 770](https://peps.python.org/pep-0770/) (in `.dist-info/sboms/`) should be the **priority**.

The `pyarrow` wheel contains both dynamically and statically linked libraries. The statically linked ones in particular will hardly be found by any scanner, which only sees the `.so` files. It is much easier to record that information at build time, even with a small Python script. See the Groovy script in apache/hadoop#8707, which does this for shaded dependencies and records the original artifact as a `pedigree` ancestor.

For example, `pyarrow` 26.0.0 contains OpenSSL 3.6.2 in three shared libraries (`libarrow`, `libarrow_flight` and `libarrow_s3`). Since upstream OpenSSL has no PURL for now, it can be identified by its CPE. A CycloneDX component entry for `libarrow` could look like this, with the same nested component repeated under the other two libraries:

```json
{
  "type": "library",
  "bom-ref": "pyarrow/libarrow.so.2600",
  "name": "libarrow.so.2600",
  "version": "26.0.0",
  "hashes": [
    {
      "alg": "SHA-256",
      "content": "e1a8b86b6bf2ad5710fbab814b8cfccbbf72b6774b7a9774ed6a0d6c9709fb77"
    }
  ],
  "evidence": {
    "occurrences": [
      {
        "location": "pyarrow/libarrow.so.2600"
      }
    ]
  },
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "library",
      "name": "openssl",
      "version": "3.6.2",
      "cpe": "cpe:2.3:a:openssl:openssl:3.6.2:*:*:*:*:*:*:*",
      "pedigree": {
        "ancestors": [
          {
            "type": "library",
            "name": "openssl",
            "version": "3.6.2",
            "cpe": "cpe:2.3:a:openssl:openssl:3.6.2:*:*:*:*:*:*:*"
          }
        ],
        "notes": "Statically linked into libarrow.so.2600. Built from the upstream OpenSSL 3.6.2 release."
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
